@inject AppSession Session
@inject PaletteSwitch Palette
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@implements IDisposable

@* The same four destinations the tab bar makes primary, in the same order, with everything else
   behind the same disclosure. Twelve links inline needed 1242px of a 1265px row at 1280px, so the
   theme button dropped to a second line there and the whole nav wrapped at every width below --
   well above the 1057px where the tab bar takes over, which was supposed to be the point at which
   this nav stopped having to cope.

   Matching the tab bar rather than picking a roomier desktop split: which four destinations are
   the daily ones does not change with the width of the screen, and a nav whose contents reshuffle
   between a laptop and a phone is one you have to re-learn. The ORDER matches it too, Progress
   before Packs -- see the note in TabBar for why that way round. *@
<nav class="app-nav">
    @* The pack from the app icon beside the name. Drawn in PackMark so the centre tab on a phone
       shows the same mark from the same path data.

       NOT called .mark, which is what it was called first. Bootstrap ships `.mark, mark` for the
       highlight element -- `padding: .1875em`, a colour, and a background of --bs-highlight-bg --
       so the class dressed this glyph in a pale yellow box in light and an olive one in dark, padded
       it, and overrode its ink. It looked like a broken asset rather than a class collision, which
       is exactly how .fold-hint's own name was arrived at further down the stylesheet. *@
    @* The wordmark goes home, which is the collection: it is what the app's own root route shows,
       and what everything else on the bar is an answer about.

       "collection" rather than the bare root, even though the root serves the same page. The nav
       entry beside this one points at "collection" and matches on the whole path, so the root is a
       URL where the collection renders and nothing in the nav lights up -- landing there via the
       wordmark would look like a page with no home. That gap is the root's and predates this link;
       pointing at the path the nav already agrees on keeps this out of it.

       A plain anchor, not a NavLink: a wordmark that filled in like a pressed button whenever you
       were on the collection would read as a sixth destination in the row. *@
    <a class="brand" href="collection" title="Your collection">
        @* Filled, where the same drawing is an outline everywhere else. A line drawing of a pack
           is the same weight as the line drawings on the buttons beside it, which is what made the
           name disappear into the row; a solid one is a different kind of mark rather than a
           bigger one of the same kind. It keeps currentColor, so it still takes the bar's ink in
           either theme. *@
        <PackMark Class="pack-mark" Filled="true" />
        PackProphet
    </a>
    @* The same marks the tab bar puts on the same five destinations, from the same component.
       They were a phone-only thing, which left the two navs agreeing about what they contained and
       disagreeing about how it looked -- and the mark is the part you learn, so learning it on a
       phone and not finding it on a laptop is the re-learning this nav exists to avoid.

       Smaller than the tab bar's 22px: there the tab IS the target and the label is a caption
       under the mark, here the word is the target and the mark sits beside it. A glyph the size of
       the word would compete with it. *@
    @* A plain anchor, and the one entry in this row that is not a NavLink.
       ==================================================================================
       The collection answers to two URLs -- the root and /collection -- and NavLink matches one
       href, so it cannot be active at both. See Routes.IsCollection for what that cost: the root
       rendered the collection with nothing lit up in either nav. Computing this one keeps a single
       source of truth for it, where adding `active` alongside a NavLink would have two things
       deciding the same class. *@
    <a class="nav-item @(AtCollection ? "active" : null)" href="collection">
        <NavIcon For="collection" Size="15" />Collection
    </a>
    <NavLink class="nav-item" href="progress">
        <NavIcon For="progress" Size="15" />Progress
    </NavLink>
    <NavLink class="nav-item" href="log">
        @* The booster from PackMark, as the wordmark beside it and the centre tab both use. *@
        <PackMark Size="15" />Log
    </NavLink>
    <NavLink class="nav-item" href="packs">
        <NavIcon For="packs" Size="15" />Packs
    </NavLink>
    <button class="nav-item nav-more-btn" popovertarget="desk-more">
        <NavIcon For="more" Size="15" />More
        <span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span>
    </button>

    @* The hotkey alone would be invisible, and on a phone there is no Ctrl+K, so the palette gets a
       button. The shortcut is printed on it rather than hidden in a tooltip. *@
    <button class="palette-btn ms-auto" @onclick="() => Palette.Toggle()"
            title="Jump to a card, set, deck or action (Ctrl+K)" aria-label="Open the command palette">
        <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" focusable="false">
                <circle cx="7" cy="7" r="4.4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" />
                <path d="M10.4 10.4L14 14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" />
            </svg>
        </span>
        <span class="hint">Ctrl K</span>
    </button>

    @* Switching collections is frequent enough to belong in the chrome; creating and deleting live
       on the Settings page. Hidden entirely with one collection. *@
    @if (Session.MultipleProfiles)
    {
        <select class="profile-switch" @onchange="PickProfile"
                title="Active collection" aria-label="Active collection">
            @foreach (var profile in Session.Profiles)
            {
                <option value="@profile.Id" selected="@(profile.Id == Session.ActiveProfileId)">
                    @profile.Name
                </option>
            }
        </select>
    }

    @* One cycling button rather than three, so the nav survives a phone width. The label names the
       current setting and the tooltip names what a tap will do. *@
    <button class="theme-btn" @onclick="CycleTheme"
            title="Theme: @Label(Session.Theme). Tap for @Label(Next(Session.Theme))."
            aria-label="Theme: @Label(Session.Theme)">
        @* Drawn, not typed. The half-filled circle for "auto" was U+25D1, which plenty of system
           fonts do not carry, so it fell back to a tofu box. *@
        <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" focusable="false">
                @switch (Session.Theme)
                {
                    case "light":
                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="3.2" fill="currentColor" />
                        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round">
                            <path d="M8 1.2v1.8M8 13v1.8M1.2 8h1.8M13 8h1.8" />
                            <path d="M3.2 3.2l1.3 1.3M11.5 11.5l1.3 1.3M12.8 3.2l-1.3 1.3M4.5 11.5l-1.3 1.3" />
                        </g>
                        break;
                    case "dark":
                        <path d="M10.8 1.8a6.2 6.2 0 1 0 0 12.4 7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-12.4z" fill="currentColor" />
                        break;
                    default:
                        @* Half light, half dark: it follows the system. *@
                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                        <path d="M8 2a6 6 0 0 1 0 12z" fill="currentColor" />
                        break;
                }
            </svg>
        </span>
        <span class="theme-label">@Label(Session.Theme)</span>
    </button>
</nav>

<MoreMenu Id="desk-more" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the collection is the page we are on, at either of its two URLs.
    /// </summary>
    private bool AtCollection => Routes.IsCollection(Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri));

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Session.Changed += OnChanged;

        // The four NavLinks below keep their own active state in step with the URL; the anchor
        // above cannot, because its state is computed during render. Without this it would be
        // correct on first paint and stale on every navigation after it.
        Nav.LocationChanged += OnLocationChanged;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Session.Changed -= OnChanged;
        Nav.LocationChanged -= OnLocationChanged;
    }

    private void OnLocationChanged(object? sender, LocationChangedEventArgs e) =>
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    private void PickProfile(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Value?.ToString() is { Length: > 0 } id) Session.SwitchProfile(id);
    }

    // auto -> light -> dark -> auto. Auto is first as the default; the explicit modes override the
    // OS.
    private static string Next(string theme) => theme switch
    {
        "auto" => "light",
        "light" => "dark",
        _ => "auto"
    };

    private Task CycleTheme() => Session.SetThemeAsync(Next(Session.Theme));

    private static string Label(string theme) => theme switch
    {
        "light" => "Light",
        "dark" => "Dark",
        _ => "Auto"
    };

}
